  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "cgNUOGrrbJwBlIIUEIRDIU1TwqI=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:22:58.769Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "These leads have no single named tool of their own; the paired fit finding records that they assemble a stack per client. The demo therefore cannot be pre-bound to a product, and the research text does not try: pick one tool from their published stack, have a webhook or hook dispatch a typed operation, then delete the source record and replay it from Powerhouse. The text states the reason the shape survives that indeterminacy \u2014 the demo is stack-agnostic because the gap is.\n\nThe practical consequence is that tool selection moves from research output to conversation input. For an integrator whose stack is chosen per engagement, asking which system the current client runs is a normal opening question rather than a gap in preparation, and the answer determines the hook without changing anything else about the demonstration.\n\nThe cohort is 20 leads, weighted to DE, FR and IT with single entries in BE, CH, ES and AT. None is priority 1: 16 sit at \"3 \u00b7 later\" and four are unprioritised. So this is a demo shape that is cheap to reuse against a cold cohort, and its value is that it costs nothing extra rather than that it unlocks anything immediately.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (20)\n\n**3 \u00b7 later**\n- Cloudogu GmbH (DE Braunschweig) \u2014 https://cloudogu.com\n- Hybird (FR Marseille) \u2014 https://hybird.org\n- Intevation GmbH (DE Osnabrueck) \u2014 https://intevation.de\n- Inuits (BE Ghent/Antwerp +CZ/PL/UA) \u2014 https://inuits.eu\n- Irontec (ES Bilbao + Madrid) \u2014 https://irontec.com\n- Kieselstein ERP eG (DE) \u2014 https://kieselstein-erp.org\n- Linagora (FR Paris/Toulouse) \u2014 https://linagora.com\n- Materna (DE Dortmund) \u2014 https://materna.de\n- Nethesis (IT Pesaro) \u2014 https://nethesis.it\n- Puzzle ITC (CH Bern) \u2014 https://puzzle.ch\n- SORINT (IT + ES/UK/DE/FR/RO/PL) \u2014 https://sorint.com\n- Smile (Groupe Smile) (FR Paris + 20 EU sites) \u2014 https://smile.eu\n- Sourcesense (IT Rome/Milan/Turin +4) \u2014 https://sourcesense.com\n- UPONU GmbH (DE (city unverified)) \u2014 https://uponu.de\n- Worteks (FR) \u2014 https://worteks.com\n- fairkom (AT (city unverified)) \u2014 https://fairkom.eu\n\n**unprioritised**\n- Limbas GmbH (DE Oberhaching (Munich)) \u2014 https://limbas.com\n- Nexedi (FR) \u2014 https://nexedi.com\n- Structr GmbH (DE Frankfurt + Dortmund) \u2014 https://structr.com\n- XWiki SAS (FR Paris) \u2014 https://xwiki.com\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Pick one tool from their published stack and show the same move: a webhook or hook dispatching a typed operation, then delete the source record and replay it from Powerhouse. The demo is stack-agnostic because the gap is.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:22:58.769812Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:22:58.769817Z"
    }
  },
